2. SOURCE-BASED SEMANTIC FINDINGS

The collector is actual public orchestration, not a new kernel primitive.
Its five top-level public functions collect, validate the supplied report,
project plain data, render canonical JSON, and provide the CLI. The report
retains typed values, callable objects, validation events and input bindings.
The immutable execution contract identifies required public producers, lanes
and validation modes; it does not store produced values or the causal map.
Validation iterates the exact 181 row keys and 235 primary receipts, checks
lane/ordinal/type/ref/callable and standalone/enclosing evidence, then derives
forward parents and reverse consumers from retained/supplied bindings. The
f78a producer-basis and 27bf causal-map digests follow those comparisons.
This is bounded in-process evidence, not cryptographic proof that an arbitrary
caller could never fabricate a Python object graph.

The first informational path has no memory-descent result and accepts the
existing Root shortcut certificate. The action-shaped query is explicitly
refused by DRS with drs_action_intent_shortcut_forbidden. High-risk routes
select full_fractal independently under the two row005 query-derived local
transactions; the shared request token is only their parent correlation.
Row063 wrappers bind each route decision, source decision artifact and selected
subject. The parent MultiRoot envelope does not create cross-Root authority.

Eight direct Root decisions occupy rows020(two),033,082,128,137,152,163.
The supplied-report validator checks the request, contribution, normalized
claim, evidence, review packet, Post-V&V candidate, GT selection, decision
input/result and transaction for each chain. Row083 retains structural and
supplier Root-context validation. Row099's construction validator is its
enclosing row103 inspection, not a fabricated standalone validator.

The delta changes one authoritative fact payload hash. Changed source IDs are
